# When Does Entropy Minimisation at Test Time Collapse?
### *A Dynamical Analysis with Stable Updates for Continual Shift*

[![Python 3.11](https://img.shields.io/badge/python-3.11-blue.svg)](https://www.python.org/)
[![PyTorch 2.5](https://img.shields.io/badge/pytorch-2.5%2Bcu121-red.svg)](https://pytorch.org/)
[![License: MIT](https://img.shields.io/badge/License-MIT-yellow.svg)](LICENSE)

---

## Abstract
Test-Time Adaptation (TTA) algorithms such as TENT adapt neural networks on unlabeled streaming test data by minimizing sample prediction entropy $\mathbb{E}_x[H(p_\theta(\cdot|x))]$. While effective on short isolated batches, under continual or changing distribution shifts, entropy minimisation suffers from catastrophic collapse: the model maps every test input to the same arbitrary class.

This project delivers:
1. **A Dynamical Analysis**: Mathematical and numerical characterization of the "rich-get-richer" gradient dynamics on a tractable Gaussian mixture linear-softmax model.
2. **Basin of Attraction Mapping**: Mapping the threshold of initial prediction bias separating stability from single-class collapse.
3. **Information-Theoretic Stabilization**: Proving that marginal entropy regularisation $-\lambda H(\bar{p})$ restores asymptotic stability around the uniform-marginal fixed point, with a computable maximum stable step size $\eta^*$.
4. **Empirical Validation**: Benchmarking on long continual streams of CIFAR-10-C (15 corruptions, severity 5) across multiple seeds.

